WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.240
For years, Android development was split between two worlds – standard React Native component

2
00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:10.760
trees on one side, and Native Jetpack Compose UI on the other.

3
00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:16.200
Today, we are connecting these environments by building a native 5-star rating bar component

4
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:21.400
entirely in compose and controlling it through React Native JSX.

5
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:26.560
Before we start, you'll React Native project must have the new architecture enabled, as the

6
00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:29.920
fabric renderer is required for this integration.

7
00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:34.920
While this involves several layers of code, the process is largely automated by code gen,

8
00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:37.520
and can be completed in six steps.

9
00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:42.480
This approach allows you to use any modern Android view in your React Native app.

10
00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:47.440
Because updates move through the C++ layer, the system avoids the serialization overhead

11
00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:50.200
that slowed down the previous architecture.

12
00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:53.480
We start by creating a TypeScript specification.

13
00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:58.520
This acts as a blueprint, strictly defining the props our component needs, like a numeric

14
00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:00.240
value for the rating.

15
00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:05.440
In step two, we run the code gen script in the terminal to process that specification.

16
00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:09.720
This generates several Java files, including a View Manager interface.

17
00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:15.040
These files serve as a contract, ensuring the native side matches our TypeScript definitions.

18
00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:17.720
For step three, we write the compose UI.

19
00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:22.800
To make it compatible with React Native, we wrap our composable code inside an abstract compose

20
00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:23.800
view.

21
00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:26.440
This view runs in its own isolated context.

22
00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:30.440
You can use native material three themes here without affecting any of your global React

23
00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:31.840
Native styles.

24
00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:34.720
Here is where many developers run into an issue.

25
00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:39.440
Fabrics layout engine, yoga, cannot automatically determine the dimensions of a jetpack compose

26
00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:40.440
view.

27
00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:43.840
This wireframe shows how yoga sees an unmeasured component.

28
00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:48.480
To fix this, we must explicitly override the measure function in Kotlin and set the exact

29
00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:51.760
dimensions so the view doesn't collapse to zero pixels.

30
00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:57.040
By providing these measurements, we ensure the native compose view takes up its intended

31
00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:00.320
space within the React Native layout tree.

32
00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:04.600
In step four, we implement the generated View Manager interface.

33
00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:10.040
This class is responsible for connecting our React props to the internal compose state.

34
00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:15.800
In the new architecture, prop updates no longer rely on sending JSON strings across a bridge.

35
00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:19.680
The C++ layer now calculates the difference between props.

36
00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:25.200
That identifies a value changed from three to four, calling the Kotlin setter directly.

37
00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:28.720
This setter updates a mutable state of variable.

38
00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:33.320
Compose observes the state change, recomposing only the affected UI.

39
00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:35.080
Step five is registration.

40
00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:38.840
We add the View Manager to the Android main application package.

41
00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:43.120
If this step is missed, the app will fail to recognize the component at runtime.

42
00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:48.400
Finally, in step six, we import the component into our JavaScript code and wrap it so it can

43
00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:51.120
be used like any other JSX element.

44
00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:57.760
By removing the need for JSON serialization, this direct pipeline between C++ and Kotlin

45
00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:01.160
allows for much more responsive UI rendering.

46
00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:06.520
Now when a state change happens in React, the compose UI updates instantly, and our native

47
00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:09.080
integration is finally complete.

48
00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:14.240
We have successfully embedded a native Jetpack Compose component into React Native, utilizing

49
00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:17.640
direct C++ prop dipping for communication.

50
00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:21.880
For those who want to explore how shadow trees and yoga layouts work under the hood,

51
00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:26.120
the React Native masterclass provides a deeper look at the fabric renderer.

52
00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:30.320
In our next session, we'll look at how to monitor these new components using debugging tools

53
00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:33.680
like Flipper and the Android Studio Profiler.

54
00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:37.760
Mastering fabric components allows you to move past framework constraints and build with

55
00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:40.280
the full power of the Android native toolkit.

